diff --git a/docs/web-bridge.md b/docs/web-bridge.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..25a2d9e
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/web-bridge.md
@@ -0,0 +1,99 @@
+# Web build: the tutorial bridge
+
+A page that embeds the web build in an `<iframe>` can drive and read the editor with
+`postMessage`. Tutorials use it to set up a lesson's file, check each step as the learner works,
+point at controls and layers, and show a step's solution. It is the control channel
+([control-protocol.md](control-protocol.md)) over `postMessage` instead of a loopback socket, with a
+smaller method list.
+
+Code: `apps/figcraft-web/src/bridge.rs` (messages), `apps/figcraft-web/src/policy.rs` (what is
+allowed, with tests), `crates/ui-egui/src/landmarks.rs` (named screen rectangles).
+
+## Messages
+
+The page posts to the iframe's window:
+
+```js
+frame.contentWindow.postMessage({ type: "figcraft", id: 1, method: "document.inspect", params: { depth: 2 } }, "https://design.example.com");
+```
+
+and receives, posted to its own origin only:
+
+```js
+{ type: "figcraft", id: 1, ok: true, result: { … } }
+{ type: "figcraft", id: 1, ok: false, error: "message" }
+```
+
+`id` is anything the page chooses; it comes back unchanged. Requests run at the start of the
+editor's next frame, in order. The data may be an object or a JSON string.
+
+Post `bridge.hello` until it answers to know the app has started:
+
+```js
+{ type: "figcraft", id: "hello", method: "bridge.hello" }
+→ { type: "figcraft", id: "hello", ok: true, result: { version: 1, methods: [ … ] } }
+```
+
+## Methods
+
+| Method | What it does |
+|---|---|
+| `bridge.hello {}` | The bridge version and its methods |
+| `engine.execute {command, params?}` | Run a command (see `engine.commands`; refused commands below) |
+| `engine.commands {}` | Every command with its parameters |
+| `document.inspect {depth?, allPages?}` | The document as JSON, selection and undo history included |
+| `node.get {id, depth?}` | One layer and its children |
+| `ui.inspect {}` | Interface state: tabs, tool, panels, dialogs, focus, status |
+| `ui.set {…}` | Switch tabs and panels (as in the control protocol) |
+| `ui.landmarks {nodes?}` | Where things are on screen (below) |
+
+### `ui.landmarks {nodes?: [id, …]}`
+
+```json
+{
+  "landmarks": { "toolbar.frame": [x0, y0, x1, y1], "design.layout.add": [ … ], "layer.12": [ … ] },
+  "nodes": { "12": [x0, y0, x1, y1] },
+  "canvas": [x0, y0, x1, y1],
+  "zoom": 1.0
+}
+```
+
+Rectangles are in logical points from the iframe's top left, which are CSS pixels at the
+browser's default zoom. Only landmarks drawn in the last frame are listed, so a panel section
+that isn't showing is simply absent. Names so far:
+
+- `panel.left`, `panel.right`, `toolbar`, `toolbar.modes`, `toolbar.mode.{draw,design,prototype,inspect}`
+- `toolbar.<group>` for each tool group button (`toolbar.move`, `toolbar.frame`, `toolbar.shape`, …)
+- `layer.<id>` for each layer row visible in the layers panel
+- `design.layout.header`, `design.layout.add` / `design.layout.remove`, `design.layout.direction`,
+  `design.layout.dimensions`, `design.layout.alignment`, `design.layout.spacing`
+- `design.position`, `design.appearance`, `design.typography` (text selected), `design.fill`, `design.stroke`,
+  `design.effects`: whole Design panel sections, clipped to what the panel shows
+
+Add more with `crate::landmarks::mark(ctx, name, rect)` where a control is drawn.
+
+## Safety
+
+- **Origins.** Only pages on the origins in `policy::ORIGINS`, on `http://localhost` or
+  `http://127.0.0.1` (any port), or listed in the `FIGCRAFT_BRIDGE_ORIGINS` build variable
+  (comma-separated, read at compile time) get an answer. Everything else is ignored silently.
+- **Methods.** No input injection (`ui.pointer`, `ui.key`, `ui.type`, `ui.input`), windows,
+  screenshots or `app.quit`.
+- **Commands.** `engine.execute` refuses sharing and live sessions (`collab.*`), plugins, saving
+  and exporting files, opening links and `app.*`, also inside `command.batch` steps.
+- At most 32 requests wait at once; a message is at most 4 MiB.
+
+## Hosting
+
+Serve the build as in [HOSTING](../packaging/web/README.md), and let the tutorial's site frame
+it: don't send `X-Frame-Options`, or send a `Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors` that lists
+the tutorial's origin.
+
+Build the site (it lands in `dist/web`), with any extra origin your tutorial runs on allowed:
+
+```sh
+cd apps/figcraft-web
+FIGCRAFT_BRIDGE_ORIGINS="https://learn.example.com" trunk build --release
